--TEST--
A `[db.<name>] path` resolves against the directory of the file it was written in, so a block in an included file names a database beside that file and not beside the running program — ADR 0103 § 5, `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The block that names this database lives in `conf/db.toml`, one directory
// away from the program and from the root configuration alike. `path` is the
// bare name `main.db`, so § 5's rule and the two ways of getting it wrong all
// answer a different file, which is what makes this case decide anything.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
var $made = $db->execute("create table t (id integer primary key)", []);

// Both sides of the bound, because either half alone reads plausibly. A
// resolver that did nothing would put the file beside the program; so would one
// that resolved against the *root* configuration's directory, which is the same
// directory here on purpose — only the block's own file is a third answer.
echo "beside the block: ", Core\IO::exists("conf/main.db") ? "y" : "n", "\n";
echo "beside the program: ", Core\IO::exists("main.db") ? "y" : "n", "\n";
--FILE conf/db.toml--
# § 5: a relative path is relative to the file it was written in. This one, not
# the root `nvs.toml` that included it.
[db.main]
driver = "sqlite"
path = "main.db"
--FILE nvs.toml--
# § 3: a config-written path is covered by `db.connect` alone — there is no
# address, and nothing here is program-supplied.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["main"]

# Only so the case can look at where the file landed.
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]

[[include]]
path = "conf/db.toml"
--EXPECT--
beside the block: y
beside the program: n
